{:edb/db-before-t 2622 :edb/db-after-t 2623 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535935 50 #inst "2026-10-15T09:24:26.464Z" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186054316 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186054317 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186054318 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186054319 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186054320 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544132 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544375 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544419 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544470 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544563 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054315 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544132 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544810 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544890 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186054835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544833 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186055513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186055513 1071 "Find $\\mid z \\mid$ if $z =-c - d\\textrm{i}$." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186055513 1071 "Find $|z|$ if $z=-c-d\\text{i},$ where $c$ and $d$ are real numbers." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186055513 1074 "For a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=-c-d\\text{i},$ we have $a=-c$ and $b=-d.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|-c-d\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{(-c)^{2}+(-d)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186059679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544842 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186061644 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544108 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186061644 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544108 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062546 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062547 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062548 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062550 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544176 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544344 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544399 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186062545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544582 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186062545 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544176 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544809 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544791 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544878 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544886 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064281 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544803 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064283 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544798 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544867 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544871 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064303 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544852 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064304 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544805 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544739 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544769 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064577 1071 "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$2{x}^{2}+7x+6=(x+\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064577 1071 "Factor the following quadratic expression.$2x^{2} + 7x + 6 =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186064577 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+7x+6.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2\\times 6=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(2,6),\\,(3,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(3,4)$ add to $7:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+4 & =7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $3x+4x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+6 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3x+4x+6 & = \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+3x)+(4x+6) & = \\\\ x(2x+3)+2(2x+3) & = \\\\ (x+2)(2x+3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064748 1071 "$\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}}=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186064748 1071 "What is $\\frac{z^{18}}{z^{7}}$?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186064748 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}} & = \\\\ {z}^{(18-7)} & = \\\\ {z}^{11} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544826 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544830 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544841 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544831 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544893 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544891 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544801 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186068889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544887 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186072542 1078 #edb/ref 17592186072543 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186073895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544874 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186073896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544885 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186073897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544785 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186074384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186074384 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $?$ |\n| $?$ | $35$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186074384 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $12$ | $$? |\n| $$? | $35$ |" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186074384 1074 "We start with the ratio $4:7$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($4$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($7$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $12,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 3}{7\\times 3}=\\frac{12}{21}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $35,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 5}{7\\times 5}=\\frac{20}{35}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $21$ |\n| $20$ | $35$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $21$ and $20.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087573 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=2+7\\textrm{i}$." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087573 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=2+7\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087573 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/da/da91da7425e6a02a202881eb1db3f1049d3f9cd204c0c1793c30e2d95676783b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n0< \\arg(z)< \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nFurther,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg (z) &= \\arctan (\\frac{7}{2}) \\approx 1.29\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087573 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/da/da91da7425e6a02a202881eb1db3f1049d3f9cd204c0c1793c30e2d95676783b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<      \\arg(z)<      \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})≈1.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087574 1071 "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=-1+\\textrm{i}\\sqrt{3}$." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087574 1071 "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=-1+\\textrm{i}\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087574 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd35c5350e1dc1988cf98b8598a350701ef0b2a02a053b97ae2c89726de50c6.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}< \\arg(z)< \\pi\n$$\n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan α &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\\nα &= \\arctan (\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087574 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd35c5350e1dc1988cf98b8598a350701ef0b2a02a053b97ae2c89726de50c6.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}<        \\arg(z)<        \\pi.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087575 1071 "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=1-\\textrm{i}$." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087575 1071 "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=1-\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087575 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/5b/5b9ec84d8c3176c85c1b5ea546fc138231a129a11b906d786578616a1dd16cb1.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}< \\arg(z)< 2\\pi\n$$\n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\arctan\\left(1\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = 2\\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 4 =\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087575 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/5b/5b9ec84d8c3176c85c1b5ea546fc138231a129a11b906d786578616a1dd16cb1.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}<       \\arg(z)<       2\\pi.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\arctan\\left(1\\right)  = \\dfrac\\pi 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = 2\\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 4 =\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087589 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-\\sqrt{3}-\\textrm{i}$." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087589 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-\\sqrt{3}-\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087589 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/00/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\pi< \\arg(z)< \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα &= \\arctan (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) = \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186087589 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/00/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi<        \\arg(z)<        \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544844 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544850 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544847 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544870 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544879 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186087955 1050 960.0 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186088936 1064 "Consider the complex number\n\n$$\nz=3+4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nas depicted on the Argand diagram below:\n\n![](images/c8/c857172451c97c24caab685a2f52097c0ca3c83df212c46a21f4a49c3edb19ca.png)\n\nThere is a natural angle $\\theta$ that $z$ makes with the positive real axis. This angle is called the **argument** of $z$. We often denote the argument as $\\arg(z)$, or just $\\theta$. The numeric value of $\\arg(z)$ is given in radians, and we restrict the values to\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\arg(z) \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nIn the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\nθ &= \\arctan (\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.93\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\arg (z) \\approx 0.93\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.\n\n**Caution:** When you evaluate\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\non your calculator, be sure that you're in radians mode, and be sure to put parentheses around the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186088936 1064 "Consider the complex number $z=3+4\\textrm{i},$ as depicted on the Argand diagram below:\n\n![](images/c8/c857172451c97c24caab685a2f52097c0ca3c83df212c46a21f4a49c3edb19ca.png)\n\nThere is a natural angle $\\theta$ that $z$ makes with the positive real axis. This angle is called the **argument** of $z.$ We often denote the argument as $\\arg(z),$ or just $\\theta.$ The numeric value of $\\arg(z)$ is given in radians, and we restrict the values to \n\n$$\n0\\leq \\arg(z) \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.93,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore $\\arg (z) \\approx 0.93$ to two decimal places.\n\n**Caution:** When you evaluate $\\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ on your calculator, be sure that you're in radians mode, and be sure to put parentheses around the $\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186092217 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544784 13194139535