#edb/ref 17592186544612 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186102764 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544612 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186105646 1071 "Find the integral expression corresponding to the area of the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sin(5\\theta)$, represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66062568cedc1b46822dd7e0e53779482f1923d621b47d29f38141ff770b4498.png)" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186105646 1071 "Find the integral expression corresponding to the area of the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sin(5\\theta),$ represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66062568cedc1b46822dd7e0e53779482f1923d621b47d29f38141ff770b4498.png)" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186105646 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = \\sin(5\\theta)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}(\\sin (5θ))^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sin^{2} (5θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.\n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or\n\n$$\n\\sin(5\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n5\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for\n\n$$\n\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right]\n$$\n\nNotice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186105646 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} 