l is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186106148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186106148 1071 "![](images/01/01a371be4c5a555b0d3790431111468acfed1471d16c4c4c305bcfe311ce3576.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sin (3θ)$, represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186106148 1071 "![](images/01/01a371be4c5a555b0d3790431111468acfed1471d16c4c4c305bcfe311ce3576.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186106148 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186107022 1064 "The area bounded by a polar curve\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta)\n$$\n\nand the rays\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\theta_1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\theta_2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\,\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve\n\n$$\nr=\\sin(3\\theta)\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9059613162af33791e7edb7413dfbac9739cf24eeabd01b0033acba2b772a.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0$, and since\n\n$$\nr=\\sin(3\\theta)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\,\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3\n$$\n\nand the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π/3}\\sin^{2} (3θ)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{0}^{π/3}(1 - \\cos (6θ))dθ \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{4}(θ\\right|_{0}^{π/3} - \\left.\\frac{1}{6}\\sin (6θ)\\right|_{0}^{π/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{π}{3} + 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186107022 1064 "The area bounded by a polar curve $r = r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta = \\theta_1$ and $\\theta = \\theta_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\, .\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9059613162af33791e7edb7413dfbac9739cf24eeabd01b0033acba2b772a.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0,$ and since $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3,$ and the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186107126 1064 "When working with polar coordinates, we primarily use the following domain for the polar angle $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, it's sometimes a good idea to use the domain\n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\lt \\pi\n$$\n\nChanging the domain for $\\theta$ turns out to be especially useful when a polar petal spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186107126 1064 "When working with polar coordinates, we primarily use the following domain for the polar angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, it's sometimes a good idea to use the domain \n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\lt \\pi.\n$$\n\nChanging the domain for $\\theta$ turns out to be especially useful when a polar petal spans the first and fourth quadrants. \n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110494 1071 "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3$." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186110494 1071 "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110494 1074 "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n\\log_{2} (2^{x + 1}) &= \\log_{2} (3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_b \\left(b^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\log_{2} (3) \\\\\nx &= \\log_{2} (3) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\log_2 \\left(3 \\right) - 1\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186110494 1074 "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110495 1071 "Given that $3^{2x+3}=5$, find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186110495 1071 "Given that $3^{2x+3}=5,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110495 1074 "Taking $\\log_3$ of both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3^{2x+3} &= 5 \\\\[3pt] \\log_3 \\left(3^{2x+3}\\right) &= \\log_3 (5) \\\\[3pt] 2x+3 &= \\log_3 (5). \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (\\log (5))/(\\log (3)) \\\\\n2x &= (\\log (5))/(\\log (3)) - 3 \\\\\nx &= (\\log (5))/(2\\log (3)) - \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx -0.768\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** We used the change of base formula to rewrite the right-hand side, as many calculators can only compute base $10$ logarithms." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186110495 1074 "Taking $\\log_3$ of both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n   \t3^{2x+3} \t\t\t\t&= 5 \\[3pt]\n\t\\log_3 \\left(3^{2x+3}\\right)\t&= \\log_3 (5) \\[3pt]\n\t2x+3 \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_3 (5) . \\[3pt]\n}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx -0.768\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** We used the change of base formula to rewrite the right-hand side, as many calculators can only compute base $10$ logarithms." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110496 1071 "Given that $3\\cdot 6^{x} = 9$, find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186110496 1071 "Given that $3\\cdot 6^{x} = 9,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110496 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 6^{x} &= 9 \\\\\n6^{x} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log_{6}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6^x &= 3 \\\\[3pt] \\log_6 \\left(6^x\\right) &= \\log_6 (3) \\\\[3pt] x &= \\log_6 (3) \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\log(3)}{\\log(6)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.613\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186110496 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {6}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {6}^{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log_{6}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n   \t6^x \t\t\t\t\t&= 3 \\[3pt]\n\t\\log_6 \\left(6^x\\right)\t&= \\log_6 (3) \\[3pt]\n\tx \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_6 (3)\\[3pt]\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\log(3)}{\\log(6)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.613\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544786 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544814 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544846 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544881 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544836 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186110848 1050 600.0 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186111879 1064 "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}$. So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} (2^{x}) &= \\log_{2} (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left({\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5)\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186111879 1064 "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123189 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123190 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123191 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123192 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123193 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544109 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544286 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544404 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186123188 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544423 1