mann sums, and \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186129423 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $L(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x-y+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x-y+2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}=f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j})={x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2\n$$\n\nand the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186129652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186129652 1071 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{y}^{2}e^{-x^{2}}dxdy$." 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186129652 1071 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186129652 1074 "![](images/79/79326f0f463d3b26db4ab497045fa35aa5aa7fb68be6fd79b5ebd633a4d6646f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/44/4452d48cac6bd387e8f7f27a9aedfbe51f9c5e478a5288f889aa3378c4805d34.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-{e}^{-4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186129855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128611 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186129855 1071 "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [-6,-1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186129855 1071 "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [-6,-1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186130379 1064 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a positive, continuous function defined on a closed and bounded rectangle $R,$ and let $P = P_1\\times P_2$ be a partition of $R.$ We know that the volume $V$ bounded by the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ in the $xy$-plane satisfies\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq V \\leq U(f,P),\n$$\n\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the lower and upper Riemann sums of $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\nImagine that our initial partition $P$ consists of a few rectangles only. Let's visualize the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, as shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89ad0fd33f34cfad4f55fc2085360878460df92479f5f938ecd254420457f3f4.png)\n\n \n\nThen, we refine $P$ by adding more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ resulting in more rectangles in $P{:}$ \n\n![](images/56/5635f58e19c47d6a9cfeab49c298ce5ca7aa1d8697e1b53390bb3bc31859637c.png)\n\n \n\nRefining the partition once again, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b2/b22f267ecf28627404eb90928e44d59b2873e88adc22ffaaa0a1a3335fffa307.png)\n\n \n\nAs we continue to refine $P$ by adding more and more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ we find that the values of the lower and upper Riemann sums approach the volume $V{:}$\n\n- $L(f, P)\\to V$ from below, and\n\n- $U(f, P) \\to V$ from above.\n\nWe define the **double integral** of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186130379 1064 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a positive, continuous function defined on a closed and bounded rectangle $R,$ and let $P = P_1\\times P_2$ be a partition of $R.$ We know that the volume $V$ bounded by the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ in the $xy$-plane satisfies\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq V \\leq U(f,P),\n$$\n\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the lower and upper Riemann sums of $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\nImagine that our initial partition $P$ consists of a few rectangles only. Let's visualize the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, as shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89ad0fd33f34cfad4f55fc2085360878460df92479f5f938ecd254420457f3f4.png)\n\nThen, we refine $P$ by adding more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ resulting in more rectangles in $P{:}$ \n\n![](images/56/5635f58e19c47d6a9cfeab49c298ce5ca7aa1d8697e1b53390bb3bc31859637c.png)\n\nRefining the partition once again, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b2/b22f267ecf28627404eb90928e44d59b2873e88adc22ffaaa0a1a3335fffa307.png)\n\nAs we continue to refine $P$ by adding more and more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ we find that the values of the lower and upper Riemann sums approach the volume $V{:}$\n\n- $L(f, P)\\to V$ from below, and\n\n- $U(f, P) \\to V$ from above.\n\nWe define the **double integral** of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186138512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544873 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186151941 1071 "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$ Further, let $P$ be a p