gin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite down the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P).$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186152153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544816 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186152153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544856 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186152154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544787 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186152232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544799 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186152232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544875 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186152327 1050 420.0 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186152716 1071 "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5y-x+3.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186152716 1071 "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5y-x+3.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 false] [17592186153351 1064 "We know how to write the lower Riemann sum for a function $f(x,y)$ over a *fixed* rectangular partition. In this lesson, we will learn how to write down an expression for the lower Riemann sum for an *arbitrary* rectangular partition. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and define the function $f(x,y)$ as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nFurther, let $P$ be an arbitrary partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find an expression for $L(f, P),$ the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition. We proceed identically to the case where $P$ was fixed.\n\nFirst, we consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nSince we're computing the *lower* sum, we're interested in the *minimum* value of $f$ in this subregion.\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $x$ increases on $[0,1],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $y$ increases on $[0,1].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nTherefore, the *minimum* value of $f$ in the subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value of $f$ at the *bottom left corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nSo, if $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion, we have\n\n$$\nm_{ij} = f(x_{i-1}, y_{j-1}) = x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$ \n\nSince the area of $R_{ij}$ is $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j,$ the volume of the rectangular solid with base $R_{ij}$ and height $m_{ij}$ is\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j = (x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j \\, .\n$$\n\nFinally, summing up all of our rectangular solids over the entire partition $P$, we find that the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j \\, .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186153351 1064 "We know how to write the lower Riemann sum for a function $f(x,y)$ over a *fixed* rectangular partition. In this lesson, we will learn how to write down an expression for the lower Riemann sum for an *arbitrary* rectangular partition. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and define the function $f(x,y)$ as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nFurther, let $P$ be an arbitrary partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find an expression for $L(f, P),$ the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition. We proceed identically to the case where $P$ was fixed.\n\nFirst, we consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nSince we're computing the *lower* sum, we're interested in the *minimum* value of $f$ in this subregion.\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $x$ increases on $[0,1],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $y$ increases on $[0,1].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nTherefore, the *minimum* value of $f$ in the subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value of $f$ at the *bottom left corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](imag