#edb/ref 17592186544959 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544781 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544961 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544781 1084 #edb/ref 17592186544979 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544781 1085 "![](images/49/49ead9d9cf872e2e5086ec6a7b2c612b70443e0275d65e969215271478d24d36.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544781 1086 #uuid "f2d248c5-c3a9-5d9c-bcfc-f830bcac9594" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544781 1087 #edb/ref 17592186544955 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544723 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544782 1069 "q-133228" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544782 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the given polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSlope $=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544782 1072 #uuid "00bb583b-38c4-5b10-945e-a15928b12671" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544782 1074 "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544749 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544783 1069 "q-75336" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544783 1071 "Let $R=[3,13]\\times [3,7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=12{x}^{2}+19{y}^{2}+3.$ Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544783 1072 #uuid "04a3375f-8916-5fd6-a307-59704d7589ef" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544783 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=12{x}^{2}+19{y}^{2}+3$ increases as $x$ increases on $[3,13],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y)=12{x}^{2}+19{y}^{2}+3$ increases as $y$ increases on $[3,7].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/6e/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=12{x}_{i}^{2}+19{y}_{j}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(12{x}_{i}^{2}+19{y}_{j}^{2}+3)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $12{x}_{i}^{2}+19{y}_{j}^{2}+3.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544650 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544764 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544784 1069 "q-133225" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544784 1071 "Let $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ represent the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of a point on the given curve. Write expressions for $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ in terms of $\\theta .$\n\n$x(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$y(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544784 1072 #uuid "04c21b11-d6eb-5e2f-9a68-02bdca097cfd" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544784 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use \n\n> $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ and $\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$\n\nwhere $x,$ $y,$ and $r$ are functions of $\\theta .$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544659 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544785 1069 "q-59864" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544785 1071 "Gerry, a gardener, is planting roses. He knows he needs $1$ cup of water for every $4$ seeds he plants. So, Gerry creates a ratio table to determine the ratio of water to seeds for different quantities.\n\n| Water (cups) | Seeds |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $?$ |\n| $12$ | $48$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nHow many seeds can Gerry plant with $7$ cups of water?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544785 1072 #uuid "07105131-b758-5e72-bdb4-02c98035c4fe" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544785 1074 "We start with the ratio $1:4$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the number of cups of water ($1$), and the denominator represents the number of seeds ($4$).\n\nWe can make a numerator of $7$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $7$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1\\times 7}{4\\times 7} & =\\frac{7}{28}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| Water (cups) | Seeds |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $28$ |\n| $12$ | $48$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, Gerry can plant $28$ seeds with $7$ cups of water." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544674 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544786 1069 "q-100163" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544786 1071 "Find the exact solution of the equation ${7}^{x+4}=5.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544786 1072 #uuid "07d6770c-e373-5366-b946-966ce2aa6000" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544786 1074 "First, we take ${log}_{7}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+4} & =5 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡({7}^{x+4}) & ={log}_{7}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+4={log}_{7}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & ={log}_{7}⁡(5) \\\\ x & ={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544779 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544787 1069 "q-164125" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544787 1071 "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=16-2x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544787 1072 #uuid "086ea7e4-b45d-5b48-92bf-b7fd8532a3af" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544787 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=16-2x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=16-2x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/4a/4a10c8332a5f346d393c37531c13db6aa69d4cecb241efd7c34d57f32d5a98b8.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=16-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(16-{x}_{i}-{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $16-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544707 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544788 1069 "q-83516" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544788 1071 "![](images/14/140947c29667712d4a1071305396fff56a24f0a5c11f26e31368b8cc011f2863.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544788 1072 #uuid "0e2ee0d0-657b-565c-90c3-ae6c29ac6b1e" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544788 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 4=8$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=8\\cdot A=12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544719 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544789 1069 "q-275971" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544789 1071 "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(x,w)={w}^{T}x-4‖w{‖}^{2}-10\n$$\n\nthat maps two vectors to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544789 1072 #uuid "1203c4f3-0f74-5bfe-84b7-69d8fde66e20" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544789 1074 "Using the results \n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)=x,\\,{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)=2w,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x-4‖w{‖}^{2}-10) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)-4\\cdot {∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}^{2})-{∇}_{w}(10) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)-4\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)-{∇}_{w}(10) \\\\  & =x-4\\cdot 2w+0 \\\\  & =x-8w.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544750 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544790 1069 "q-132483" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544790 1071 "On a webpage, visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $15$ seconds.  Determine the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than $0.5$ minutes.  Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544790 1072 #uuid "1455aec3-db48-5efe-b5bf-59b8688bf438" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544790 1074 "Since visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next visitor appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between two consecutive visitor arrivals. Since visitors arrive on average every $15$ seconds, the rate is $\\lambda =60÷15=4$ visitors per minute.\n\nThe time between two consecutive visitor arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(4).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than \n$0.5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}{|}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544651 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544791 1069 "q-101733" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544791 1071 "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ |" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544791 1072 #uuid "14e3fc47-0564-56b4-b83c-8a7fa94d57dd" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544791 1074 "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=f(0)=-1,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-2,0].$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544747 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544792 1069 "q-265744" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544792 1071 "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(w)=-3‖w{‖}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nthat maps a vector to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544792 1072 #uuid "17d82828-3c8a-5034-bf42-fab4e2f3cbb6" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544792 1074 "Using the result \n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)=2w,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}(-3‖w{‖}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}^{2})-{∇}_{w}(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)-{∇}_{w}(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2w+0 \\\\  & =-6w.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544773 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544793 1069 "q-125695" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544793 1071 "![](images/17/172737d1a5b28b9a60958dc7e6b1748db1912021b7df4ad26045648063037fd8.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544793 1072 #uuid "1ca5b3d9-2cf9-5f0b-b261-8040bf2d2224" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544793 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{8}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544686 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544794 1069 "q-96576" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544794 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X=-18)$ can be approximated as" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544794 1072 #uuid "1e025671-c988-5444-b52c-2cc6ba70eb20" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544794 1074 "We construct an interval around $X=-18$ with a radius of $0.5:$\n\n![](images/9c/9ccbf2335f4db52e60760d4740ebf29ac054b6b249b6f0436e02fbff3311d7d4.png)\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=-18) & ≈P(-18-0.5\\le Y\\le -18+0.5) \\\\  & =P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544685 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544795 1069 "q-68721" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544795 1071 "$10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544795 1072 #uuid "1fb4db83-3d4f-5c8b-938d-159db1a747a1" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544795 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=10\\cdot \\frac{8}{125}=\\frac{80}{125}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{80}{125}=\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25\\cdot 5}=\\frac{16}{25}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544675 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544796 1069 "q-332026" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544796 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-3)}{s(s-5)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544796 1072 #uuid "229cdf1d-b683-5de3-b351-27b1d6a26233" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544796 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=2{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-2){X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)=4 \\\\ 2{X}_{1}(s)+(s-3){X}_{2}(s)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)[\\frac{-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)}{2}]+3{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ (s-2)[-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)]+6{X}_{2}(s) & =8 \\\\ -2(s-2)-[(s-2)(s-3)-6]{X}_{2}(s) & =8 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-5s){X}_{2}(s) & =8+2(s-2) \\\\ -s(s-5){X}_{2}(s) & =2s+4 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-2(s+2)}{s(s-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-(s-3)\\cdot \\frac{-2(s+2)}{s(s-5)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2s(s+5)+2(s-3)(s+2)}{2s(s-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2s-3)}{s(s-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-3)}{s(s-5)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-2(s+2)}{s(s-5)}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544661 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544797 1069 "q-68718" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544797 1071 "${(\\frac{3}{7})}^{3}=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544797 1072 #uuid "2545d131-b88a-533a-a3f0-2d6b5ef7d401" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544797 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{3}=\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${7}^{3}=343,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}=\\frac{27}{343}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544728 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544798 1069 "q-133226" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544798 1071 "Find the derivative of $y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544798 1072 #uuid "2749929e-e01c-5706-82d9-161e96c84d4c" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544798 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\ny(\\theta )=6(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $y(\\theta )$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=6{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=12u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & =12u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544708 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544799 1069 "q-75352" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544799 1071 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [2,9]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=11y-6x.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544799 1072 #uuid "27a8dfe2-6a96-5884-8d1c-c8b7f914c19a" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544799 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=11y-6x$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=11y-6x$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c4/c4aa9e96fe72a8fb31ab62ff7eb0695cc64992bb9433be516461915a5c92baff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544721 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544800 1069 "q-125349" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544800 1071 "![](images/94/9405ee6a4b24ef36c2a59a9f4a984728716e9c281456d1459d591393d3c73720.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n$r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )},$\n  represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544800 1072 #uuid "2be045cc-480f-57a5-b39f-c617f83c588d" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544800 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal lies in the fourth quadrant. Since the petal lies in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta +\\pi $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta +\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,4\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},-\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},-\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies in the fourth quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544673 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544801 1069 "q-73781" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544801 1071 "Which of the following values of $w$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{w}{4}<5\\,?$\n\nI. $w=24$\n\nII. $w=20$\n\nIII. $w=8$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544801 1072 #uuid "3861e4f2-43a8-5a8e-aa2e-58c7272cde17" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544801 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=24$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{4} & <5 \\\\ \\frac{24}{4} & <5 \\\\ 6 & <5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=24$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=24$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=20$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{4} & <5 \\\\ \\frac{20}{4} & <5 \\\\ 5 & <5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=20$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=20$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=8$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{4} & <5 \\\\ \\frac{8}{4} & <5 \\\\ 2 & <5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=8$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=8$ **satisfies** this inequality." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544663 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544802 1069 "q-125357" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544802 1071 "![](images/d9/d9985c6afd3e5440dbe1e51e4046e7744c9a2e01472354054796247d40ed7ed6.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n$r=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$\nrepresented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544802 1072 #uuid "3922fac2-663d-5efa-bc44-c819bbafb745" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544802 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544701 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544803 1069 "q-133227" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544803 1071 "Find the derivative of $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,$  with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544803 1072 #uuid "39c6a843-fd29-52a1-8f7b-ab703e4af195" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544803 1074 "First, we write our function as the composite function\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=2\\theta .\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544692 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544804 1069 "q-127800" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544804 1071 "![](images/44/446278a820ba21da7f5a1f15639e8cc4f96e38f38f3b5905b926bb9d2a106948.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544804 1072 #uuid "3aec8613-8bc5-55d8-bb63-25dfe3e5bcce" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544804 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544718 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544805 1069 "q-34432" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544805 1071 "If $\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{-6/5}}={z}^{k},$ then $k=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544805 1072 #uuid "3b874545-de9e-5368-9969-5a0f4efc79db" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544805 1074 "First, we express the radical as a fractional power and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{-6/5}} & = \\\\ {({z}^{-6/5})}^{1/3} & = \\\\ {z}^{(-6/5)(1/3)} & = \\\\ {z}^{-2/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-\\frac{2}{5}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544752 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544806 1069 "q-123856" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544806 1071 "Let $R=[-3,1]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}-{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544806 1072 #uuid "3c4eccd2-7dd0-53ca-8b67-e044fcc594a5" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544806 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}\\le \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})\\le {y}_{j}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{j-1}+{x}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}-(-3)^{2})(4-2)=-8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})(1-(-3))=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 24-(-8)\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 32\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=32.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544652 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544807 1069 "q-275973" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544807 1071 "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(w)=‖w{‖}_{1}-{w}^{T}w\n$$\n\nthat maps a vector to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$ You may assume that $w$ has all nonzero components." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544807 1072 #uuid "3cb041fc-343a-591c-abe2-c70938440309" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544807 1074 "Using the results\n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})=\\text{sign}(w),\\,{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)=2w,\n$$\n\n we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1}-{w}^{T}w) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})-{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w) \\\\  & =\\text{sign}(w)-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544715 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544808 1069 "q-140954" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544808 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544808 1072 #uuid "3d35be6f-b8bf-5aae-b526-fffd6ac155d1" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544808 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544731 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544809 1069 "q-52181" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544809 1071 "$-{z}^{3}(4{z}^{2}-2z)=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544809 1072 #uuid "3eaa9b96-57f5-5bea-bcf0-0afa03bea744" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544809 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{z}^{3}(4{z}^{2}-2z) & = \\\\ (-{z}^{3})(4{z}^{2})+(-{z}^{3})(-2z) & = \\\\ ((-1)\\cdot 4)({z}^{3}\\cdot {z}^{2})+((-1)\\cdot (-2))({z}^{3}\\cdot z) & = \\\\ (-4){z}^{3+2}+(2){z}^{3+1} & = \\\\ -4{z}^{5}+2{z}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544705 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544810 1069 "q-70259" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544810 1071 "What is the magnitude of the number $z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}?$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544810 1072 #uuid "407ccba6-df92-5ced-bae5-a0584a2e9a9b" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544810 1074 "The imaginary number $3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}$ is equivalent to the complex number $z=0+3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$\n\nWe know that for a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of $z=0+3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|z| & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544682 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544811 1069 "q-143573" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544811 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{2x+3} & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x+1\\le y\\le x+2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y<2\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}).$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544811 1072 #uuid "427792fe-a52b-5a35-ba4b-43ed95fe05d5" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544811 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<2\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1+x}^{2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1+3/4}^{2}\\frac{2y}{(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+3)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{7/4}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[{y}^{2}]_{7/4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(4-\\frac{49}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544751 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544812 1069 "q-82254" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544812 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544812 1072 #uuid "437b2f48-85ba-532a-9ac5-52a828cb51ba" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544812 1074 "![](images/47/472aa6b0afe20f7c1902446102265f758637c9ec00eb985bf38a658dc92f3fe5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2-x\\,⟹\\,x=2-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/77/77b59ce5c40514338b0d23a31e7028c7fa0f7343aad018576301fcc4ec441ac1.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544678 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544813 1069 "q-83484" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544813 1071 "![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544813 1072 #uuid "43e3bf66-f5d8-57b0-9e72-5dc370f879ec" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544813 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544681 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544814 1069 "q-66801" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544814 1071 "Find the exact solution of the equation ${3}^{x}=5.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544814 1072 #uuid "4dbd2fd5-279c-5b4f-83af-6acbee35f908" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544814 1074 "We take ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =5 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx={log}_{3}⁡(5)\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544726 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544815 1069 "q-130722" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544815 1071 "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544815 1072 #uuid "4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544815 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544762 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544816 1069 "q-75306" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544816 1071 "Let $R=[3,4]\\times [-5,-1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544816 1072 #uuid "50a1adba-14a0-5a88-abaf-07e0387210e6" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544816 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=2{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $2{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544711 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544817 1069 "q-125863" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544817 1071 "![](images/66/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544817 1072 #uuid "50affdee-0a40-5255-b196-53de7a7824c6" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544817 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $7$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=0,$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0 \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{7},\\frac{3\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{3\\pi }{7},\\frac{4\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{5\\text{th}}\\,{{[\\frac{4\\pi }{7},\\frac{5\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{7},\\frac{6\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{7\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544765 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544818 1069 "q-132566" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544818 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544818 1072 #uuid "5502822c-28cd-5be6-838d-dcd2d5622d7d" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544818 1074 "![](images/31/314b881b95ea804456b3e41a1d8fec70cf459e007e2120d9018e3bef02f1b523.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1,\\,{e}^{y-1}\\le x\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y-1}\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9b097164e2b5215a051e18de055f3cce4c26600caf510bc3b0071b77b645089.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544657 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544819 1069 "q-280718" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544819 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,2,k⟩$, $b=⟨-3,0,4⟩$, and $c=⟨2,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544819 1072 #uuid "58e03782-f890-5578-a853-4a903526c725" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544819 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|+k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0\\cdot 5-4\\cdot (-1))-2((-3)\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 2)+k((-3)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4-2(-15-8)+3k \\\\  & =50+3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 50+3k & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{50}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{50}{3}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544729 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544820 1069 "q-360575" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544820 1071 "![](images/6f/6feb80156311c92f948b35742b39df3e6582c62a0d578481026b3141b01de3f1.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n$r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$\n represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544820 1072 #uuid "5a2aaba8-489e-5805-b9f6-0c2a5c67d926" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544820 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $6\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}].\n$$\n\nNotice that this interval matches the angular region traced by the shaded petal in the diagram.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544666 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544821 1069 "q-133231" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544821 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to our polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nThe tangent line is $y\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544821 1072 #uuid "5bcca525-fc41-52cc-be8b-f245ac9162a9" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544821 1074 "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544770 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544822 1069 "q-170684" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544822 1071 "Let $R=[-1,3]\\times [-4,-2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=8y-2x-5.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544822 1072 #uuid "5cf5fafd-606c-53b3-9dcd-de496f0b8804" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544822 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=8y-2x-5$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=8y-2x-5$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/74/74d546d91e145efea49b70af56cb472e8bbbd1417725cd7fa92a3a278cad75ea.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j})=8{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(8{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}-5)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $8{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}-5.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544667 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544823 1069 "q-145091" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544823 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({y}^{2}+x), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544823 1072 #uuid "5ffa5cf0-3e04-5a20-bfc9-964d09fea30a" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544823 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{8}({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{3x}{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{16}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{12}{16}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}({y}^{2}+1), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8}({y}^{2}+x))}{(\\frac{3}{4}({y}^{2}+1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+x}{2({y}^{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}+x}{2({y}^{2}+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544745 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544824 1069 "q-123747" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544824 1071 "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [3,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=4y-2x+3.$ Further, let \n\n$$\nI={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\,\\le I\\,\\le U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544824 1072 #uuid "60f150de-1cdb-534e-a48d-461dca7bdb52" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544824 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $L(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=4y-2x+3$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=4y-2x+3$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}=f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+3\n$$\n\nand the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+3)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+3.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544713 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544825 1069 "q-124682" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544825 1071 "![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544825 1072 #uuid "623b63a4-baae-557b-86dd-761ba0b029fe" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544825 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544691 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544826 1069 "q-74708" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544826 1071 "Evaluate $f(-2,1)$ if $f(x,y)=3x+y-1.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544826 1072 #uuid "6705c0b1-618f-5fe7-bac1-95db3808efcf" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544826 1074 "We substitute $x=-2$ and $y=1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =3x+y-1 \\\\ f(-2,1) & =3(-2)+(1)-1 \\\\  & =-6+1-1 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544735 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544827 1069 "q-75335" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544827 1071 "Let $R=[3,4]\\times [1,9]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}-21.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544827 1072 #uuid "673e501d-d4fb-5f61-85b5-c2fe793fdded" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544827 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}-21$ increases as $x$ increases on $[3,4],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}-21$ increases as $y$ increases on $[1,9].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/6e/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=3{x}_{i}^{2}+8{y}_{j}^{2}-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(3{x}_{i}^{2}+8{y}_{j}^{2}-21)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $3{x}_{i}^{2}+8{y}_{j}^{2}-21.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544725 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544828 1069 "q-125494" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544828 1071 "![](images/a0/a010feba178f2db834e1939a91c81fd95131d65b10c7616bd92140fa46eaa305.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544828 1072 #uuid "68c240a3-d1c2-54cd-a001-e4698ed241e7" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544828 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544704 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544768 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544778 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1069 "q-133232" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1071 "![](images/f6/f6cac98f2c8c7cf182ef5cd88019c12a8b8c6ad8e52d0a01f8174332b366eb7e.png)\n\nThe upper half of our polar curve can be expressed in Cartesian coordinates as $y=\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3.$ Write an integral expression that gives the exact area $A$ of the region enclosed between the curve and the  $x$-axis, as shown above.\n\n$A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1072 #uuid "68d6c745-8c8f-5b38-826d-b15655a70429" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544829 1074 "We have the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3\n$$\n\nand the limits of integration $x=-3$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the required area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544720 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544830 1069 "q-130578" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544830 1071 "Evaluate $f(1,1)$ if $f(x,y)=(3y-x)^{3}+(2x-y)^{2}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544830 1072 #uuid "6e8aaddd-953c-576c-ad6b-2e4d36b66988" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544830 1074 "We substitute $x=1$ and $y=1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =(3y-x)^{3}+(2x-y)^{2} \\\\ f(1,1) & =(3(1)-1)^{3}+(2(1)-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544654 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544694 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544831 1069 "q-223793" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544831 1071 "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $-7{d}^{2}=-49d$ are $d=${{field-1}} and $d=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544831 1072 #uuid "7228245a-4caf-5105-bd90-6c52f7ce3f5f" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544831 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{d}^{2} & =-49d \\\\ -7{d}^{2}+49d & =0 \\\\ -7d(d-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\n-7d=0\\,\\text{or}\\,d-7=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nd=0\\,\\text{or}\\,d=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $d=0$ and $d=7.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544669 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544832 1069 "q-127796" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544832 1071 "![](images/19/192007bbd5aee67607897bc1e4e3e29d106c8f6041fe8b06025f48999e68fcfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544832 1072 #uuid "77bce5e4-123e-5ff9-af9f-15d1cb34dfd9" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544832 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $9\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n9\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544717 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544833 1069 "q-177595" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544833 1071 "If $-3=\\frac{a}{9},$ then $a=${{field-1}}." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544833 1072 #uuid "7841e502-bd87-5f16-9f68-b66396ece51e" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544833 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{9}=-3\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $a$ is being divided by $9.$ To isolate $a$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $9.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $9$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{9} & =-3 \\\\ 9\\cdot \\frac{a}{9} & =9\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{9a}{9} & =-27 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{9}a}{\\cancel{9}} & =-27 \\\\ a & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544737 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544834 1069 "q-14916" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544834 1071 "![](images/f6/f62e6a4cec5b2a877f2491a101f3929edd6664424b86daab4bbfe8096c5c9282.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544834 1072 #uuid "790dcc10-7f22-59d7-b4bc-0f6943a2af9d" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544834 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544766 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544835 1069 "q-275978" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544835 1071 "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(x,w)={(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3}-4{x}^{T}w\n$$\n\nthat maps a vector to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$ You may assume that $w$ has no zero components." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544835 1072 #uuid "7dc41e95-539c-50b9-8fb0-b63764e8684c" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544835 1074 "Using the results\n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})=\\text{sign}(w),\\,{∇}_{w}({x}^{T}w)=x,\n$$\n\n we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}({(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3}-4{x}^{T}w) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}({(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3})-{∇}_{w}(4{x}^{T}w) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}({(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3})-4\\cdot {∇}_{w}({x}^{T}w) \\\\  & =3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot {∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})-4\\cdot x \\\\  & =3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4\\cdot x \\\\  & =3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544688 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544836 1069 "q-31891" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544836 1071 "Given that ${5}^{2a+3}-13=25,$ find the value of $a$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544836 1072 #uuid "7e1ee8c2-d8a9-5564-8fe2-55ce248be31a" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544836 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3}-13 & =25 \\\\ {5}^{2a+3} & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{5}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3} & =38 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2a+3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(38) \\\\ 2a+3 & ={log}_{5}⁡(38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ 2a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\na≈-0.370\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544756 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544837 1069 "q-124414" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544837 1071 "![](images/51/51ab11405aeb635dafaeadef1b174db163f395a62126225f99de07884b3d8cb9.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544837 1072 #uuid "7f3edede-f2b8-5413-b0b9-835c57755de4" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544837 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544757 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544838 1069 "q-351293" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544838 1071 "![](images/c8/c8afd0a30a2796cc1590e97c8d71e1d47128ac1993d2875072877149631642e5.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544838 1072 #uuid "7f4b1de5-2584-553e-ad73-4df20f726215" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544838 1074 "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{10}.$\n\n![](images/ca/caacb78b0f37dcc58d6568a571c99bc569c7eb95aeacf724d5df33e35ba21fa7.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544754 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544839 1069 "q-83792" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544839 1071 "![](images/f3/f3196a19057d5a5f658faad1ef028b12a12b87de56f93dc3f2df1aa03f21fcf2.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544839 1072 #uuid "809efbd7-39b4-57dd-a5fb-9c26fa380f5b" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544839 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $7\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544683 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544840 1069 "q-127997" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544840 1071 "![](images/0b/0befc6c40eb7b53a45f1e27d0445d5abc614937c37b36d5cbf5420e1d2b51ff9.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n$r=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$\n represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544840 1072 #uuid "841c4177-3882-5def-83ad-75d73368e31f" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544840 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{24},\\frac{5\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{5\\pi }{24},\\frac{5\\pi }{24}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /24}^{5\\pi /24}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544760 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544841 1069 "q-130574" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544841 1071 "Evaluate $f(2,1)$ if $f(x,y)=(x-3y)(x+y)^{2}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544841 1072 #uuid "850f2473-aeb8-5637-89c4-7aa5503f51fe" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544841 1074 "We substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =(x-3y)(x+y)^{2} \\\\ f(2,1) & =(2-3(1))(2+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544743 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544842 1069 "q-133233" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544842 1071 "Given $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ find the third derivative of $r(\\theta )$ with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${r}^{(3)}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544842 1072 #uuid "88c04c0e-97a3-5196-b229-a35ba120f617" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544842 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{(3)}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544763 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544843 1069 "q-83485" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544843 1071 "![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544843 1072 #uuid "8b33c7a8-87dc-53ea-9478-54f9a2f3e5e0" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544843 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544662 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544844 1069 "q-319490" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544844 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=5+12\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544844 1072 #uuid "9165f710-3b4c-5eb0-a90d-63ff961aee99" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544844 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=arctan\\,(\\frac{12}{5})≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544677 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544845 1069 "q-125329" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544845 1071 "![](images/94/94a5da0925a82b91d345fa7811e41c219c19ca48d76b30927e920efed3b2edb3.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ shown above." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544845 1072 #uuid "9182ab7c-62c2-510f-9183-c91606315f00" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544845 1074 "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}.$\n\n![](images/7f/7f1a9ba1b4d9a86e23b8634dedaf6d4e1a651efbe4fc7aa27ac4dbb5d443018f.png)\n\nSince there are $12$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =12\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 8{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =48{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12} \\\\  & =48[(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =48(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544777 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544846 1069 "q-66811" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544846 1071 "Given that ${3}^{4-2x}=\\sqrt{2},$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544846 1072 #uuid "918c847a-dee1-5e90-aa75-e08ca9d39fd8" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544846 1074 "Taking ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-2x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4-2x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ 4-2x & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{1/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-2x=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ -2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-4 \\\\ x & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈1.842\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544664 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544847 1069 "q-319578" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544847 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=6-6\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544847 1072 #uuid "91c2517c-65da-5c92-ac2d-49406e18b196" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544847 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/7c/7cdd04a6728120c66f4befafeb5a0bf2f053c8ed0ed2c1014e87549820e5c3ac.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544722 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544848 1069 "q-351393" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544848 1071 "![](images/21/219480e656f2f5913d7a83d93871c09b334600365d46a041ae63607d3f2f8326.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544848 1072 #uuid "950e52f2-cc86-53ef-af25-6a46ca25d518" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544848 1074 "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\n![](images/e1/e1f3651f2c120b65063015a17a7e657ccb5b2e87df475660785d35748c05557a.png)\n\nSince there are $8$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =8\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =32[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =32(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544714 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544849 1069 "q-127794" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544849 1071 "![](images/b7/b7475f0a169e62121711d52daaf51e8954053fc35738266d94240b6e70de6258.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544849 1072 #uuid "96dc50ad-7c40-5c2b-a752-a6f1cca71c8d" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544849 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions 