iven by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544851 1072 #uuid "99966b73-9392-5920-a350-678f41228cda" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544851 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-7{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[1,2],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-7{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[0,1].$\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/5b/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=30-7{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(30-7{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $30-7{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544759 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544852 1069 "q-86657" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544852 1071 "Given that $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{n},$ what is $n?$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544852 1072 #uuid "99da1bf2-2ba4-5686-8f97-30d5bccdda0f" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544852 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} & = \\\\ {({x}^{3})}^{1/5} & = \\\\ {x}^{3\\cdot (1/5)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $n=\\frac{3}{5}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544658 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544853 1069 "q-133229" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544853 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for our polar curve.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544853 1072 #uuid "9ab9373f-f442-5c71-ba1a-50adb5530bb2" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544853 1074 "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544733 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544854 1069 "q-75346" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544854 1071 "Let $R=[2,7]\\times [-13,-5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=4x-7y+8.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$\nwhat is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544854 1072 #uuid "9c397521-c62f-5e70-81fc-4938ad811121" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544854 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=4x-7y+8$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=4x-7y+8$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/01/01b291ca7cd81b88cf6ed06fdcdb2ffcbb1f78255d0f57d496823d347c2fb0b3.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/de/de36b521dea1f925754a9c630c3d4a2c405c087a191138f1eaaa5771becb8c21.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=4{x}_{i}-7{y}_{j-1}+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i}-7{y}_{j-1}+8)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4{x}_{i}-7{y}_{j-1}+8.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544716 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544855 1069 "q-73747" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544855 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(-1,1,1)$, $B(2,-2,1)$, $C(1,2,2)$, and $D(2,-1,4).$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544855 1072 #uuid "9ce41098-082c-5659-8a15-6d5205341359" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544855 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-2j+k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =3i-3j \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+2j+2k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i-j+4k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =3i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 1)+3(2\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1)+0 \\\\  & =15+9 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|24|=24.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544727 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544856 1069 "q-75307" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544856 1071 "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+y.$ Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544856 1072 #uuid "9d148965-74db-5cec-9065-74d36d2d9d5c" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544856 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+y$ increases as $x$ increases on $[0,2],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+y$ increases as $y$ increases on $[1,5].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=5{x}_{i-1}^{2}+2{y}_{j-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(5{x}_{i-1}^{2}+2{y}_{j-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $5{x}_{i-1}^{2}+2{y}_{j-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544702 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544857 1069 "q-100726" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544857 1071 "$(14÷9)^{4}=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544857 1072 #uuid "9d8ff61d-9e20-514e-9557-6082e8e48970" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544857 1074 "To raise the quotient of two numbers to the power of $4$, we must raise each number to the power of $4,$ and then divide:\n\n$$\n(14÷9)^{4}={14}^{4}÷{9}^{4}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544740 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544858 1069 "q-133230" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544858 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for our polar curve.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544858 1072 #uuid "9dac1de0-3399-5e2c-82ff-248d3a51fed2" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544858 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx}) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544671 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544679 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544695 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544767 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1069 "q-332388" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-2},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=-\\frac{2}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s-5}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{5t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1072 #uuid "9de0e52e-4e54-5cd5-8e58-4fda732ef76e" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544859 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\cdot t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{2}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s-5}\\} \\\\  & =-2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-5}\\} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2\\cdot t}+{e}^{5\\cdot t} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2t}+{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{2t}+{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544741 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544860 1069 "q-128009" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544860 1071 "![](images/cc/cc41b37639eb7715fbecd55510222f77957c05b705bc83b93c755ad2e7cf3dcd.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n$r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$\n  represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544860 1072 #uuid "a11f42c6-9c97-5af9-adee-a7210e67b825" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544860 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,4\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{24},\\frac{\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{11\\pi }{24},\\frac{\\pi }{24}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-11\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544670 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544861 1069 "q-68731" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544861 1071 "${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544861 1072 #uuid "a48af0b4-b2cb-5194-ba80-dd04350d6f6a" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544861 1074 "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(-2)}{3}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}={(\\frac{(-2)}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{4}=16$ and ${3}^{4}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}=\\frac{16}{81}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544710 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544862 1069 "q-124424" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544862 1071 "![](images/97/97b30cfa241a6e1b9db4d5332749ae503a926711c0e7c78ab188c2327463c89b.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544862 1072 #uuid "a9a8bb7e-ad2c-5185-87eb-ce30902a04e7" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544862 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544649 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544863 1069 "q-123850" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544863 1071 "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [2,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(8-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(8-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544863 1072 #uuid "ac9755ca-22ae-5d63-a731-5137b2daeebf" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544863 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n8-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}\\le 8-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 8-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n8-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}\\le 8-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 8-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[8-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le 8{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})(3-2)=4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{2}-{2}^{2})(2-0)=5 \\\\ 8{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =8(2-0)(3-2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 16-4-5\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 7\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=7.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544732 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544864 1069 "q-133234" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544864 1071 "Let $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and let ${P}_{2}(\\theta )$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $r(\\theta )$ about $\\theta =0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(\\theta )$ is used to approximate $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.2).$ Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$|{R}_{2}|\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544864 1072 #uuid "acc186f8-9dad-51c7-bad3-42fb2258f011" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544864 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ about $\\theta =a$ to approximate $r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|\\theta -a{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $\\theta =0.2.$\n\nPlugging $\\theta =0.2$ and $a=0$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.2{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to maximize $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|$ on the interval $[0,0.2].$ Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that for $\\theta \\in [0,0.2],$ we have\n\n$$\n|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|=|-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta |=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.2],$ its value is greatest at $\\theta =0.$ So,\n\n$$\nM=|{r}^{(3)}(0)|=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{6}{6}|0.2{|}^{3} \\\\  & =(0.2)^{3} \\\\  & =0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544736 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544865 1069 "q-93129" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544865 1071 "$40$ students took an algebra exam. If $12$ of them failed this exam, what percentage of students failed it?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544865 1072 #uuid "ae08ca9c-939c-5021-b3a5-7c6bc6fdc648" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544865 1074 "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of students that failed the exam, which is $12,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of students, which is $40.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=12÷40=\\frac{12}{40}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{40}=\\frac{12÷4}{40÷4}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10}{10\\times 10}=\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $30%$ of the students failed the exam." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544753 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544866 1069 "q-99627" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544866 1071 "$\\frac{63}{4\\sqrt{7}}=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544866 1072 #uuid "af48560b-05fb-5bc9-95de-b8bf00a67a5f" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544866 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{7}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{63}{4\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{63}{4\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{63\\sqrt{7}}{4\\cdot (\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{63\\sqrt{7}}{4\\cdot 7} & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\sqrt{7}}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544724 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544867 1069 "q-2798" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544867 1071 "If ${({x}^{-3})}^{5}={x}^{k},$ then $k=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544867 1072 #uuid "b2001685-083c-5b23-92d4-dc534c9b8927" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544867 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{-3})}^{5} & = \\\\ {x}^{(-3)5} & = \\\\ {x}^{-15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-15.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544744 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544868 1069 "q-351400" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544868 1071 "![](images/dc/dcec4003e842d279dbed89307ef84a230b16443fe515a8cc621aea72bf8829d3.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544868 1072 #uuid "b75e23ab-5c6f-5e0d-91f4-1cb8debf07f9" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544868 1074 "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c35b0e3c4e51b164097e6deb11e4418726927f478f934e41d1250388e9a27e5.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{(\\theta -\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =20[(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544665 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544869 1069 "q-90869" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544869 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544869 1072 #uuid "b91bd5f9-20da-5be7-a581-8dc4f819e202" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544869 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}+1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot 1+({t}^{3}+1-1)\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544689 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544870 1069 "q-319509" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544870 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7-3\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544870 1072 #uuid "b96d4599-55b6-52a4-b5e9-744fec2eb4d7" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544870 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{7})=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544687 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544871 1069 "q-25388" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544871 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${({p}^{0}{q}^{-3})}^{-3}?$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544871 1072 #uuid "b9bd1889-37e7-5c18-aff9-d79a4155a663" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544871 1074 "First, we simplify the expression inside the parenthesis and then we apply the power rule\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({p}^{0}{q}^{-3})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {(1\\cdot {q}^{-3})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {({q}^{-3})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {q}^{(-3)(-3)} & = \\\\ {q}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544755 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544872 1069 "q-132558" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544872 1071 "The waiting time for one customer to be served at a cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one person served every $4$  minutes. Determine the probability that the next person is served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544872 1072 #uuid "bc8f4760-f931-5737-8a9c-5aab0b30c4bf" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544872 1074 "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544684 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544706 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544873 1069 "q-175481" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544873 1071 "$\\frac{1}{3}(9x+12y)=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544873 1072 #uuid "bdb96e93-5d4a-5d24-ab91-43e3876a1cf8" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544873 1074 "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(9x+12y) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9x+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 12y & = \\\\ \\frac{9x}{3}+\\frac{12y}{3} & = \\\\ 3\\,x+4\\,y & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544690 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544874 1069 "q-59913" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544874 1071 "| $5$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $?$ | $30$ |\n| $25$ | $50$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544874 1072 #uuid "c2d93ca7-606b-5dec-9244-b98215ef5117" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544874 1074 "We start with the ratio $5:10$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{10}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($5$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($10$).\n\nWe can make a denominator of $30$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 3}{10\\times 3} & =\\frac{15}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| $5$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $30$ |\n| $25$ | $50$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $15.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544693 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544875 1069 "q-123516" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544875 1071 "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=y-2x+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544875 1072 #uuid "c350d5bc-4404-57ee-b54b-285bbf9a8f0e" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544875 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=y-2x+10$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=y-2x+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c4/c4aa9e96fe72a8fb31ab62ff7eb0695cc64992bb9433be516461915a5c92baff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})={y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+10)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+10.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544734 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544876 1069 "q-82347" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544876 1071 "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544876 1072 #uuid "c3ba9c34-ab2d-5d40-bb57-ca2ed5ef7608" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544876 1074 "![](images/ab/abf9e5dda9e98caac491aa5d94b3e2ae3f08f90d6bd490762adabc1437ac49d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}-1\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](images/a4/a45e4b63b1eff23617abcbe33ce522652b20de6c817dfde2d9cdda52d12bb778.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt[3]{y+1}.$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y+1}\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544676 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544877 1069 "q-75330" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544877 1071 "Let $R=[-2,4]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=3x+4y+15.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544877 1072 #uuid "c4b9636f-8ac3-587f-891f-98d959ec0c7f" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544877 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+4y+15$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+4y+15$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/6e/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=3{x}_{i}+4{y}_{j}+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(3{x}_{i}+4{y}_{j}+15)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $3{x}_{i}+4{y}_{j}+15.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544742 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544878 1069 "q-101731" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544878 1071 "Find an interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,12\\,$ | $\\,14\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ |" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544878 1072 #uuid "c86d9a3a-c5bd-5503-9489-53c383f2b865" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544878 1074 "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(8)=f(14)=6,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[8,14].$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544771 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544879 1069 "q-8452" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544879 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-5-3\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544879 1072 #uuid "cbe4274d-84e6-566b-a678-1dfe16c416af" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544879 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/95/9596107dc58d6acb67f1014ebc17e632dd03173aa975fa84b196588db873d8e5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})≈0.540\\,42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.540\\,42≈3.68\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544655 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544680 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544700 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544761 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1069 "q-332382" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-3},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{3}{s-1}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1072 #uuid "d0737b10-0304-5e2e-a18d-ef1cf02a07ff" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544880 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3\\cdot t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{3}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}-3{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\cdot t}-3{e}^{1\\cdot t} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}-3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544774 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544881 1069 "q-973" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544881 1071 "Given that ${11}^{4-y}=\\sqrt[4]{3},$ find the value of $y$  rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544881 1072 #uuid "d436286b-8ea5-5b92-b174-4ebb9b14bf53" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544881 1074 "Taking ${log}_{11}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{4-y} & =\\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ {log}_{11}⁡({11}^{4-y}) & ={log}_{11}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{3}) \\\\ 4-y & ={log}_{11}⁡({3}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-y=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{11}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\ -y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}-4 \\\\ y & =4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈3.885\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544696 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544882 1069 "q-254581" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544882 1071 "A customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes. If a ticket has been open for $20$ minutes already, what is the probability that it will remain unresolved for at least another $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544882 1072 #uuid "d44e0172-2699-5b95-8b6b-c9e2c6a8cdf3" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544882 1074 "Since the customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next ticket is resolved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the ticket resolving time, in minutes. Since the team resolves $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}$ tickets per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{5}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5},$ $s=20,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20+10\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X>10) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/5)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.135.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544772 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544883 1069 "q-331950" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544883 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544883 1072 #uuid "d4b0fddb-f441-5332-9904-2f366a824fe8" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544883 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2={X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-4=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-1){X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s)=2 \\\\ 2{X}_{1}(s)+(s-1){X}_{2}(s)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-1)[\\frac{4-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2}]-3{X}_{2}(s) & =2 \\\\ 4(s-1)-(s-1)^{2}{X}_{2}(s)-6{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ 4(s-1)-[(s-1)^{2}+6]{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ 4(s-1)-({s}^{2}-2s+7){X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-2s+7){X}_{2}(s) & =4-4(s-1) \\\\ -({s}^{2}-2s+7){X}_{2}(s) & =-4s+8 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(s-1)\\cdot \\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({s}^{2}-2s+7)-(s-1)\\cdot 4(s-2)}{2({s}^{2}-2s+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{s}^{2}-8s+28-4(s-1)(s-2)}{2({s}^{2}-2s+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s+20}{2({s}^{2}-2s+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(s+5)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(s+5)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544697 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544884 1069 "q-265746" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544884 1071 "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(x,w)=3{w}^{T}x-‖w{‖}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nthat maps two vectors to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544884 1072 #uuid "d8c2cd33-feb8-5850-af19-6e3631c29b25" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544884 1074 "Using the results \n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)=x,\\,{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)=2w,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}(3{w}^{T}x-‖w{‖}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)-{∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}^{2})+{∇}_{w}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)-{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)+{∇}_{w}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot x-2w+0 \\\\  & =3x-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544648 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544885 1069 "q-101518" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544885 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $?$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $?$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544885 1072 #uuid "d91b69e3-fd77-5457-a2ba-3ce70f9e9f93" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544885 1074 "We start with the ratio $3:15$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{15}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($3$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($15$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $6,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 2}{15\\times 2}=\\frac{6}{30}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $10,$ we divide the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{6÷3}{30÷3}=\\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $2$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $30$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $2$ and $30.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544672 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544886 1069 "q-129415" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544886 1071 "$\\sqrt{64{p}^{2}}=$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544886 1072 #uuid "e0efe9ed-909f-5452-9389-702e59cbbd2e" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544886 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $64$ and ${p}^{2}$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{64{p}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}\\cdot {p}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot 8\\cdot p\\cdot p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot p\\cdot 8\\cdot p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8p\\cdot 8p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8p)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(8p)^{2}} & =|8p| \\\\  & =|8|\\cdot |p| \\\\  & =8|p|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544660 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544887 1069 "q-100085" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544887 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{y+7}{2}?$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544887 1072 #uuid "e1dd8d26-7afa-5e9e-b2fe-b38aed946f00" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544887 1074 "The numerator and denominator have no common factors, so the expression can not be reduced. The expression \n\n$$\n\\frac{y+7}{2}\n$$\n\n is already in lowest terms." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544738 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544888 1069 "q-143644" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544888 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6, & \\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544888 1072 #uuid "e1e5b64b-413b-5b25-a4f6-89eb28f41500" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544888 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{y}}6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot x\\,{|}_{y}^{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =6(\\sqrt{y}-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6(\\sqrt{y}-y), & \\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6(\\sqrt{y}-y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}-y}, & \\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544709 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544889 1069 "q-132552" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544889 1071 "On a busy waterway, barges pass by a particular point continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ minutes. Determine the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to four decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544889 1072 #uuid "e3898571-1060-5a54-af07-b5592b492d4f" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544889 1074 "Since barges pass continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time between two successive barges using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between two successive barges. Since a barge passes every $10$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ barges per minute.\n\nThe time interval between two successive barges can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<7) & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-7/10}+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & ≈0.1099\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544712 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544890 1069 "q-160280" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544890 1071 "What is the magnitude of the number $z=-9\\text{i}?$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544890 1072 #uuid "e440f356-fb13-5144-832e-aba59bbee006" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544890 1074 "The imaginary number $-9\\text{i}$ is equivalent to the complex number $z=0-9\\text{i}.$\n\nWe know that for a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of $z=0-9\\text{i}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|z| & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-9)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544653 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544891 1069 "q-80060" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544891 1071 "Solve the equation $2s+5{s}^{2}=s+4{s}^{2}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544891 1072 #uuid "e6e37409-094d-5a1f-ac21-68506e2e806b" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544891 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2s+5{s}^{2} & =s+4{s}^{2} \\\\ 2s+5{s}^{2}-(s+4{s}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2s+5{s}^{2}-s-4{s}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (5{s}^{2}-4{s}^{2})+(2s-s) & =0 \\\\ {s}^{2}+s & =0 \\\\ s(s+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\ns=0\\,\\text{or}\\,s+1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\ns=0\\,\\text{or}\\,s=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $s=-1,0.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544746 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544892 1069 "q-95408" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544892 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u⊥v$\n\nIII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544892 1072 #uuid "eaf43e73-9a1c-5cad-a4f5-266c54b6fab6" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544892 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the same eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\nThis can occur when the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvaule is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544698 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544703 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544893 1069 "q-223729" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544893 1071 "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $5{p}^{2}=3p$ are $p=${{field-1}} and $p=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544893 1072 #uuid "eda5f44d-20e8-5e57-ad4a-984bec77ae99" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544893 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{p}^{2} & =3p \\\\ 5{p}^{2}-3p & =0 \\\\ p(5p-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\np=0\\,\\text{or}\\,5p-3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\np=0\\,\\text{or}\\,p=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=0$ and $p=\\frac{3}{5}.$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544775 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544894 1069 "q-132486" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544894 1071 "A monitor issues a warning signal when an action is needed as part of a production process. Signals occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $0.2$ minutes.  Determine the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544894 1072 #uuid "eddfe563-295a-5298-9f24-7cdb158be9e9" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544894 1074 "Since the monitor issues a warning signal continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next signal appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between successive signals. Since the monitor issues a warning signal every $0.2=\\frac{1}{5}$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =5$ signals per minute.\n\nThe times between two successive signals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-5x}|}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544758 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544895 1069 "q-254609" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544895 1071 "While riding on a road, a cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $4$ potholes per hour. If the cyclist has not encountered any potholes in the past $30$ minutes, what is the probability that he will encounter a pothole in the next $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544895 1072 #uuid "f1e2b523-bf44-5c32-9b95-836b1e8d2e60" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544895 1074 "Since the cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next pothole as an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, until the cyclist encounters the next pothole. Since the cyclist encounters, on average, $4$ potholes per hour (or $1$ pothole every $15$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ potholes per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 30+10\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X\\le 10) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.487.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544656 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544896 1069 "q-124684" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544896 1071 "![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544896 1072 #uuid "f9ba8fdb-9397-5097-95f0-204a59ce4162" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544896 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544897 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544984 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544897 1062 #uuid "01535422-d9f2-5d71-a038-27ff6a878cc3" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544897 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544893 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544898 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544979 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544898 1062 #uuid "01f21eb4-b1a0-59f4-8a66-c8d33fde667e" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544898 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544782 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544899 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544959 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544899 1062 #uuid "02dd2953-b049-5f76-b85b-d1d16110fb37" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544899 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544829 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544900 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544966 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544900 1062 #uuid "038c7b55-7263-5198-8c54-9c42b551c2fd" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544900 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544795 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544901 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544990 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544901 1062 #uuid "041d72b6-5556-5043-94cb-bee43a50b2ba" 13194139535935 17592186045417 true] [17592186544901 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544833 13194139535935 1